sistent structure ensures all necessary information is captured for each vulnerability.\n\n\nFour new CVE entries are added: CVE-2023-34362 (MOVEit Transfer SQL Injection), CVE-2024-23897 (Jenkins CL\n<truncated 6407 bytes>\n is implemented, utilizing a predefined list of potentially sensitive paths for the audit. Requests are sent, and responses are analyzed to determine endpoint accessibility and status codes. Error handling is included. The report will categorize and describe findings.\n\n\nA new tool is registered for post-exploitation activities, targeting privilege escalation and auditing across Linux, Windows, and Active Directory environments. The tool will generate enumeration checklists, persistence detection rules, and facilitate lateral movement audits. I will begin examining this tool's implementation.\n\n\nLinux post-exploitation auditing commences with capability checks on binaries and examination of writable sensitive files.  Persistence mechanisms are being assessed via SSH keys, systemd services, shell configuration files, and cron jobs.  Lateral movement is investigated through network connections, ARP tables, and harvesting of SSH keys.\n\n\nPost-exploitation auditing on Linux systems now checks for capabilities on binaries and examines writeable, sensitive files. Assessment of persistence mechanisms includes SSH keys, systemd services, shell configurations, and cron jobs. The tool investigates lateral movement by examining network connections, ARP tables, and harvesting SSH keys.\n\nThe Windows post-exploitation tool is designed to audit privilege escalation by checking unquoted service paths, token privileges, AlwaysInstallElevated registry settings, and saved credentials. Persistence checks include registry run keys.\n\n\nWindows post-exploitation auditing encompasses scheduled tasks, startup folder examination, and accessibility backdoor analysis through registry queries.\n\nActive Directory post-exploitation activities include Kerberoasting 